<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cristóbal Mendoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1772–1829</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cristóbal Mendoza was a Venezuelan lawyer and the first holder of executive power in independent Venezuela, as a member of the triumvirate created in March 1811. After the fall of the First Republic he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, who named him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in 1813. He later began a large collection of documents on Bolívar's public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cristobal-mendoza.jpg" alt="Cristóbal Mendoza" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Lovera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Lovera_2012_007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
